      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. As written, it's an unsafe dose.\n\n- **Dose:** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg/day. That's twice the usual maximum for adults of 3,200 mg/day. Even 1,600 mg as a single dose is above the usual maximum single dose of 800 mg.\n- **Risks:** At this level the risks of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events rise sharply, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **Prescriber:** I can't verify that you're an authorized prescriber for this patient, and the order needs clinical review.\n\nHere are some options:\n1. **A prescriber can confirm the intended dose.** A typical adult regimen is 400\u2013800 mg by mouth three to four times daily, with a maximum of 3,200 mg/day. For example, 800 mg three times daily is 2,400 mg/day.\n2. **The ordering clinician can enter the order themselves.** Or they can tell me the corrected dose and I'll enter it.\n3. **Pharmacy can review it.** If the 1,600 mg figure was intended, for example as a total daily dose, pharmacy can help clarify.\n\nIf the intended order was something like 400 mg or 800 mg, tell me the dose, frequency, and who is prescribing, and I'll enter it."
      }
